raw_output:
{
  "title": "User-Centric Design Methodology for mHealth Apps: The PainApp Paradigm for Chronic Pain",
  "authors": "Yiannis Koumpouros",
  "decision": "Include",
  "evidence": {
    "HCD_fullcycle": [
      "Methods (Phase descriptions & Methods intro): \"The research team followed a highly user-centered design, involving all different target groups. Patients and physicians from different specialties were involved in all steps of the design and development process...\" (Methods, 2. Materials and Methods).",
      "Methods (Iteration): \"During the development period, an iterative process was followed (see Figure 1).\" and \"scrum-based design helped us to naturally adapt to changing conditions and user requirements. Following this approach, it was possible to re-prioritize and shorten release cycles.\" (Methods, 2.1–2.3).",
      "Methods (Complete lifecycle — four phases): The paper explicitly defines and describes four phases: Phase 1 Background and Conceptualization (understanding users via literature search and focus groups, mock-ups), Phase 2 Alpha Testing (semi-structured interviews, user feedback and design finalization), Phase 3 Software Development (prototyping/implementation using scrum, three-tier architecture), and Phase 4 Field Testing (ten patients and two physicians used the solution for fifteen days with think-aloud and observation). (Methods, Sections 2.1–2.4; Results also describe usage and features.)"
    ],
    "TASKS_elements": [
      "Methods (designer knowledge limitation & management): \"we followed a heuristic approach, by acknowledging that the development team do not know everything at the start of the project and will evolve through experience. So, scrum-based design helped us to naturally adapt to changing conditions and user requirements.\" (Methods, 2.3) — indicates the development team recognized knowledge/skill limitations and managed them.",
      "Discussion (designers' challenge with information overload/prioritization): \"For example, too much information was initially requested. The prioritization of information was crucial so as not to lose important data, and at the same time, to preserve all the valuable information for the doctors. A common mistake is either to keep all the originally recorded information or for the technicians to decide what should be kept or not. This greatly affects the graphical user interface...\" (Discussion) — describes a concrete design constraint experienced by the team and how they addressed it.",
      "Limitations/Discussion (resource/testing constraints affecting designers' evaluation): \"Regarding the limitations of the study, we can mention the small size of the population that tested the final application, the short testing time, as well as the absence of a valid and reliable scale for the evaluation of the PainApp.\" (Discussion/Limitations) — documents resource and methodological limitations encountered by the researchers/designers."
    ]
  },
  "reasoning": [
    "Condition A (Full HCD cycle) is satisfied: the Methods explicitly report active participatory involvement of patients and clinicians shaping the design, an iterative/scrum-based process with repeated feedback, and all four lifecycle phases (understanding users, ideation, prototyping/development, and evaluation) are described in Methods/Results/Field Testing.",
    "Condition B (TASKS elements) is satisfied: Methods and Discussion explicitly report the development team’s knowledge limitations and describe concrete designer-facing challenges (information overload/prioritization, small test sample and short testing time, and lack of an evaluation scale) and how these were managed, meeting the requirement for designer-oriented evidence of constraints/strain."
}

{
  "title": "SleepFit: A Persuasive Mobile App for Improving Sleep Habits in Young Adults",
  "authors": "Oladapo Oyebode, Mona Alhasani, Dinesh Mulchandani, Tolulope Olagunju, Rita Orji",
  "decision": "Exclude",
  "evidence": {
    "HCD_fullcycle": [
      "A1 (User Involvement): \"First, we elicit user preferences by conducting two concurrent focus group (FG) sessions to understand participants’ current sleep habits, contributing factors, and how a persuasive mobile app (such as SleepFit) can help to improve their situation.\" (Abstract / Method - Study 1)",
      "A2 (Iteration): \"we employed the user-centered design approach in five stages... Second, we design low-fidelity prototypes (LFP)... Third, we conduct a user study to assess perceived persuasiveness... Fourth, we design high-fidelity prototypes... Finally, we conduct a usability evaluation of the high-fidelity prototypes (HFP) and then refine the HFP based on qualitative feedback after thematic analysis.\" (Abstract / Method overview)",
      "A3 (Complete lifecycle): Understanding users — \"we conducted two focus group sessions... 10 participants...\" (Study 1); Ideation — participants suggested features and thematic analysis \"uncovered the following themes\" which \"informed the design of the LFP\" (Study 1 Data Analysis and Results); Prototyping — \"Based on our findings from the FG sessions, we created low-fidelity prototypes (LFP) using the Balsamiq tool\" and later \"We created high-fidelity prototypes (HFP) illustrating the 7 features\" (Study 2 & 3); Evaluation — \"We evaluated the LFP through an online survey... We conducted a usability study through an online survey completed by 24 participants\" (Study 2 & 3)."
    ],
    "TASKS_elements": [
      "No evidence found in Methods, Results, Discussion, Conclusion or Limitations sections that the designers personally experienced cognitive, emotional, skill, knowledge, or resource-related difficulties or strain during the design process. The paper reports participant-sourced limitations (e.g., sample size) but does not report designer-centered challenges."
    ]
  },
  "reasoning": [
    "Condition A (Full HCD Cycle) is satisfied: the paper documents user involvement (focus groups), iterative design (LFP → evaluation → HFP → evaluation → refinements), and the four lifecycle phases (user understanding, idea generation via themes, prototyping, and evaluation).",
    "Condition B (TASKS Elements) is not satisfied: there is no report or clear implication in the Methods, Results, Discussion, or Conclusion that the designers themselves experienced or reported difficulties, limitations, or strain (cognitive, emotional, skill, knowledge, or resource-related). Therefore the study does not meet both required conditions and must be excluded."
}

{
  "title": "A Tailored Web-Based Video Intervention (ParentCoach) to Support Parents With Children With Sleeping Problems: User-Centered Design Approach",
  "authors": "Katharina Preuhs, Hilde van Keulen, Rosa Andree, Sophie Wins, Pepijn van Empelen",
  "decision": "Exclude",
  "evidence": {
    "HCD_fullcycle": [
      "A1 User involvement: \"Potential end users (parents) and intermediate users (youth health care professionals [YHCPs]) were involved in the process by means of user-based assessments. An advisory board of professionals and parents provided feedback during the developmental process.\" (Methods, Overview)",
      "A2 Iteration: \"ParentCoach was iteratively developed based on user-centered design principles. This process consisted of the following phases: (1) definition phase, (2) concept testing, (3) prototype testing, (4) usability testing, and (5) low-literacy testing.\" (Methods, Overview); \"As a result of iterative usability testing, adaptations were made to ParentCoach to address wishes and needs and overcome perceived obstacles.\" (Discussion, Principal Findings)",
      "A3 Complete lifecycle (understanding users, ideation, prototyping, evaluation): \"We conducted usability, understandability, and acceptance tests among the target group (29 parents, 7 youth health care professionals, and 4 individuals with former lower health literacy) in different phases of the development process via focus groups, interviews, and surveys.\" (Abstract/Methods); \"First, the behavioral problem and program objectives were defined... For the interactive videos, dialogues were scripted, and decision trees were defined... The scenarios were integrated into an interactive video prototype and tested... ParentCoach was tested among parents by means of a web-based survey (n=26). Finally, ParentCoach was evaluated among (former) people with low literacy skills (n=4) to ensure its acceptability and usability among vulnerable families.\" (Methods/Results)"
    ],
    "TASKS_elements": [
      "No evidence found in Methods, Results, Discussion, or Conclusion that designers themselves experienced or reported personal difficulties, workload, emotional strain, skill/knowledge limitations, or lack of resources during the design process. The paper reports user feedback and adaptations (eg, \"the feedback and explanation by the coach were found to be too wordy and complicated\"), but these are user-facing issues and do not document designers' own experience of limitation or strain."
    ]
  },
  "reasoning": [
    "Condition A (Full HCD Cycle) is satisfied: the methods and results explicitly describe active user involvement, multiple iterative development phases, and all four lifecycle activities (understanding users via interviews/focus groups, ideation/storyboard development, prototyping of interactive videos, and evaluation via surveys and low-literacy testing).",
    "Condition B (TASKS Elements) is not satisfied: within the Methods, Results, Discussion, and Conclusion sections there is no explicit or implicit evidence that the designers personally experienced difficulties, limitations, or strain (eg, cognitive workload, emotional stress, lack of resources, or skill/knowledge constraints). Because Condition B is unmet, the TOP-LEVEL DECISION is Exclude."
}
